I like that concept. It’s a meaningful distinction.

A recording is a fixed sequence:

R(t)=\text{constant playback}

Every viewing is the same.

An AI play would instead be more like a generative performance:

P = f(\text{script},\ \text{world state},\ \text{audience},\ \theta,\ \epsilon)

where:

* the script provides the narrative invariants,
* the world state provides current context,
* the audience changes emphasis and pacing,
* \theta represents the performer’s learned style,
* \epsilon is controlled randomness.

Each performance is different:

P_i \neq P_j

yet preserves the same core identity:

I(P_i)\approx I(P_j)

where I(\cdot) extracts the story’s essential structure.

That is much closer to how theater or jazz works than film.

A Shakespeare play isn’t “the” performance. Every cast, every night, every audience produces a new instantiation of the same underlying work. The script acts as a compressed invariant that supports many valid realizations.

For AI, that opens interesting possibilities:

* Characters that genuinely evolve while remaining recognizable.
* Stories that respond to the audience rather than replaying verbatim.
* Worlds that stay internally consistent while generating new events.
* Rewatchability, because no two performances are identical.

In that model, the artifact you create isn’t the final output. It’s the generator of performances.

That’s a different notion of authorship. The author designs the invariant and the constraints; the AI performs a fresh realization each time. It’s closer to composing a musical score than recording a song. The score persists, while each performance becomes a unique event.